.\" lightbox.8: the manual page of lightbox.
.\"
.\" It lists the commands and their options, as "lightbox help" does, with a
.\" line or two on each. It is kept in step with the binary by hand, as
.\" README.md and the pages of docs/ are: no test reads it. The date below is
.\" that of its last change.
.TH LIGHTBOX 8 2026-11-22 lightbox "System Administration"
.SH NAME
lightbox \- minimal, secure container runtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lightbox
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR path ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B lightbox help
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B lightbox \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lightbox
turns a directory that holds a Linux root filesystem, a
.IR base ,
into containers.
.B create
makes a container from a base,
.B start
gives it namespaces, a cgroup with its limits, an address on a bridge of the
host's and a sandbox,
.B exec
runs commands in it, and
.B stop
and
.B rm
take it away again.
There is no daemon: the init of a container is lightbox itself, and what is
known about a container is in files.
.PP
A new container is sandboxed unless an option says otherwise.
It has 11 capabilities, a seccomp filter that allows a list of system calls
and answers the rest with ENOSYS, no_new_privs, a filter on the devices it may
open, and a network that reaches the internet and nothing of the host.
.PP
.BR setup ,
.BR reload ,
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR restart ,
.BR reset ,
.BR update ,
.BR exec ,
.BR run ,
.B import
and
.B base import
are run as root, and so is
.B create
with
.B \-\-userns
or
.BR \-\-profile .
The directories lightbox keeps its data in are root's alone, so in practice
every command is.
.PP
This page lists the commands and their options.
The documentation of lightbox has every value they take, what each command
prints and refuses, the settings of
.IR lightbox.conf ,
the network, the sandbox and how to run an AI agent in a box: see
.BR "SEE ALSO" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
The
.I lightbox.conf
to read.
It comes before the command.
Without it lightbox reads the file that
.B LIGHTBOX_CONFIG
names, and otherwise
.IR /etc/lightbox/lightbox.conf .
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h ", " help " [\fIcommand\fR]"
Print the usage, or one command's part of it, on standard output.
.B \-\-help
after a command does the same.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version of lightbox and of the lightc it was built with.
.SH COMMANDS
A container's name is 1 to 30 characters of a\-z, A\-Z, 0\-9, _ and \-.
A size is a number of bytes, optionally with K, M, G or T, or
.BR max .
A duration is a number, optionally with a point and up to three more digits,
and s, m, h or d; without a unit it is seconds.
.SS Making and changing containers
.TP
.BI create " name" " \fR[\fIip\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]"
Create a container from a base.
Without
.I ip
it gets the lowest free address of the subnet.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-rootfs " path"
The base to make the container from.
The default is what
.B LIGHTBOX_ROOTFS
names, then
.B rootfs
in
.IR lightbox.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-base " id"
The snapshot to make the container from, by the id that
.B base ls
shows, in the place of a base: the one
.B base import
unpacked an image into.
Not with
.BR \-\-rootfs .
.TP
.B \-\-copy
A full copy of the base, and not an overlay of its snapshot.
.TP
.BI \-\-mem " limit"
The memory limit: a size, or
.B max
for none.
The default is 256M.
.TP
.BI \-\-swap " limit"
The swap the container may use beyond its memory: 0, a size, or
.BR max .
The default is 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-pids " limit"
The most processes, from 1 to 4194304, or
.BR max .
The default is 128.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " num"
CPUs, from 0.01 to 512, such as 2 or 0.5.
The default is 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-io\-max " limit"
An I/O limit for one whole disk, as the kernel's io.max takes a line, such as
"8:0 wbps=10485760", with rbps, wbps, riops and wiops.
.TP
.BI \-\-oom\-score " n"
The OOM score adjustment, from \-1000 to 1000.
The default is 500.
.TP
.BI \-\-ttl " duration"
How long the container may run from each start.
Then it stops as
.B stop
stops it, and is
.IR expired .
.TP
.BI \-\-vol " src" : dst\fR[\fB:ro\fR]
A directory or a file of the host's in the container.
Repeatable, up to 16.
.TP
.BI \-\-workspace " dir"
A directory of the host's at
.IR /workspace ,
where every
.B exec
then runs.
.TP
.B \-\-read\-only
A root filesystem that cannot be written to.
.TP
.B \-\-userns
A user namespace of its own: the container's root is an unprivileged uid of
the host's, from the range that
.I /etc/subuid
and
.I /etc/subgid
give the name lightbox.
.TP
.BR \-\-seccomp " " allow | deny
The seccomp profile: the allow\-list, which is the default, or the deny\-list.
.TP
.B \-\-privileged
No sandbox: all capabilities, no seccomp filter and no device filter.
.TP
.BI \-\-net " mode"
What the container may reach.
.BR outside ,
the default, is the internet and its name servers only;
.B nat
the host and its networks too;
.B none
loopback alone;
.BI proxy: port
that TCP port of the gateway, where a proxy listens, and nothing else.
.TP
.BI \-\-link " name"
Allow traffic between this container and another.
Repeatable, up to 8.
.TP
.BI \-\-dns " address"
A name server for the container.
Repeatable, up to 3.
The default is the host's.
.TP
.B \-\-profile agent
A box for an AI agent's session: a user namespace, the allow\-list, an
overlay, and the network of
.B \-\-net none
unless
.B \-\-net outside
or
.BI "\-\-net proxy:" port
is given, with the limits and the lifetime of the agent_* settings.
It refuses
.BR \-\-privileged ,
.BR \-\-link ,
.BR "\-\-net nat" ,
.B \-\-seccomp deny
and
.BR \-\-copy .
.RE
.TP
.BI update " name" " \fR[\fB\-\-mem\fR \fIlimit\fR] [\fB\-\-pids\fR \fIlimit\fR] [\fB\-\-cpu\fR \fInum\fR]"
Change a container's limits: at once if it runs, and from its next start in
any case.
.TP
.BI add\-vol " name src" : dst\fR[\fB:ro\fR]
Add a volume to a container.
One that runs has it from its next start.
.TP
.BI rm\-vol " name dst"
Remove the volume at a destination.
.TP
.BI reset " name\fR... [\fB\-t\fR \fIseconds\fR]"
Make the root filesystem of containers their base's again, without a copy.
One that runs is stopped first.
Volumes are kept.
.TP
.BR rm " [" \-f "] \fIname\fR..."
Remove containers.
.B \-f
kills one that runs first.
.SS Starting and stopping
.TP
.BI start " name\fR... [\fB\-t\fR \fIseconds\fR]"
Start stopped containers, one after the other.
.B \-t
is how long to wait for the base's
.IR /init.sh :
30 seconds unless set, and 0 does not wait.
.TP
.BI stop " name\fR... [\fB\-t\fR \fIseconds\fR]"
Stop running containers, one after the other.
.B \-t
is how long their processes get to end before they are killed: 10 seconds
unless set.
.TP
.BI restart " name\fR... [\fB\-t\fR \fIseconds\fR]"
Stop containers that run, as
.B stop
does, and start them.
.SS Running commands
.TP
.BI exec " name" " \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] [\fIcommand\fR [\fIarguments\fR]]"
Run a command in a running container.
Options come before the command, and a command that begins with a dash goes
after
.BR \-\- .
Without a command,
.B exec
runs
.IR /bin/sh .
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-user ", " \-u " \fIname\fR|\fIuid\fR[\fB:\fIgid\fR]"
Who the command runs as.
The default is root.
.TP
.BR \-\-env ", " \-e " \fIKEY\fB=\fIVALUE\fR"
Set a variable in the command's environment.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-workdir ", " \-w " \fIdir\fR"
The directory the command runs in.
The default is /, or
.I /workspace
in a box with a workspace.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
End the command, and all it started, after this long.
.B exec
then exits with 124.
.RE
.TP
.BR run " [\fIcreate options\fR] [\fIexec options\fR] [" \-\- "] [\fIcommand\fR [\fIarguments\fR]]"
Create a box, start it, run a command in it as
.B exec
runs it, and remove it.
A box whose
.B run
was killed is removed by the next
.BR run .
.SS Looking at containers
.TP
.BR ls " [" \-\-json ]
List all containers, with their addresses and states.
.TP
.BI inspect " name" " \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]"
Show a container's configuration and state.
.TP
.BI logs " name" " \fR[\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-\-audit\fR]"
Show what the container has printed.
.B \-f
follows it.
.B \-\-audit
shows what
.B exec
and
.B run
asked of it, which is kept after it is removed.
.SS The host
.TP
.BR doctor " [" \-\-json "] [" "\-\-profile agent" ]
Check what lightbox needs of this host, and say how to fix what is missing.
It exits with 1 when a check fails.
.B \-\-profile agent
checks what a box of that profile needs too.
.TP
.BI export " name dir"
Write a container that is not running as an image in
.IR dir ,
which is made, or taken when it is there, empty and the caller's own: an OCI
image layout, as
.BR skopeo (1)
and
.BR oras (1)
copy one to a registry.
A container that overlays a snapshot is two layers, the snapshot and its own
files; a copy is one.
The manifest carries the container's limits, and nothing of what it may do on
a host.
The same container gives the same image, byte for byte.
.TP
.BI import " dir name \fR[\fIip\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]"
Make a container of the image in
.IR dir ,
an OCI image layout, with the options of
.B create
but
.B \-\-rootfs
and
.BR \-\-base .
The image is read first, and every blob of it held to the size and the
SHA-256 it is named by; an image that is not what it says, or is of another
architecture, is refused and nothing is made.
An image of two layers that
.B export
wrote is a container: a snapshot, which is taken only when none here is that
layer, and the container's own files.
Any other image is one snapshot.
A layer compressed with gzip is unpacked through
.BR gzip (1),
which runs as nobody, without capabilities or a network.
The limits that no option gives are the image's, where they are no more than
.I lightbox.conf
gives a new container; what is more is said and not taken, and the option
gives it.
Nothing else an image says of its container is taken.
.TP
.B base ls
List the snapshots of bases that containers overlay.
.TP
.BI "base add" " dir"
Take a new snapshot of a base, for the containers made after it.
.TP
.BI "base import" " dir"
Unpack the image in
.IR dir ,
an OCI image layout as
.BR skopeo (1)
copies one from a registry, into one snapshot, all its layers one over the
other, for
.BR "create \-\-base" .
The image is read and held to its digests as
.B import
reads one, and a snapshot that is those layers already is found.
An image's command and environment are not read.
As root.
.TP
.BI "base rm" " id\fR..."
Remove snapshots that no container uses.
.TP
.B setup
Make the host's side of the network: the bridge and lightbox's firewall
rules.
With iptables it also turns IPv4 forwarding on, unless
.B firewall
or
.B ip_forward
is false in
.IR lightbox.conf .
Every
.B start
does the same when something of it is missing.
.TP
.B setup \-\-teardown
Take lightbox's host network away, when no container runs.
.TP
.B reload
Put lightbox's firewall rules and bridge in again, and the rules of running
containers, after a reload of the host's firewall took them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed.
For
.BR doctor :
a check failed.
For a command with several names: the first status that was not 0.
.PP
.B exec
and
.B run
exit with the status of the command they ran, or with 128 plus the number of
the signal that ended it.
Their own statuses are those of
.BR env (1)
and
.BR timeout (1):
.TP
.B 124
.B \-\-timeout
ended the command.
.TP
.B 125
lightbox failed before the command ran.
.TP
.B 126
The command could not be run.
.TP
.B 127
The command was not found.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LIGHTBOX_CONFIG
The
.I lightbox.conf
to read, when
.B \-\-config
names none.
.TP
.B LIGHTBOX_ROOTFS
The base that
.B create
and
.B run
use when they are given no
.BR \-\-rootfs .
.PP
Nothing of the environment reaches a container.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/lightbox/lightbox.conf
The settings of the host.
Every setting has a built\-in value, so the file is optional.
.TP
.I /var/lib/lightbox
What lightbox keeps: the snapshots of bases in
.IR bases ,
the containers in
.IR containers ,
the audit log in
.IR audit.log ,
and the default base in
.IR rootfs .
.TP
.I /run/lightbox
The records of the containers that run.
.TP
.IR /etc/subuid ", " /etc/subgid
The ranges of ids for the name lightbox, which
.B \-\-userns
takes one of for each container.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make the host's network once, then create, start and use a container:
.PP
.RS
.EX
lightbox setup
lightbox create web \-\-mem 512M \-\-cpu 2
lightbox start web
lightbox exec web cat /etc/os\-release
lightbox stop web
lightbox rm web
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run one command in a box of the agent profile, with no network, a time limit
and the current directory as its workspace:
.PP
.RS
.EX
lightbox run \-\-profile agent \-\-workspace "$PWD" \-\-timeout 10m \-\- make test
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check the host:
.PP
.RS
.EX
lightbox doctor
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iptables (8),
.BR nft (8),
.BR namespaces (7),
.BR user_namespaces (7),
.BR cgroups (7),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR seccomp (2)
.PP
.UR https://ozkr.ac/lightbox/
The documentation of lightbox
.UE
.PP
.UR https://github.com/OzkrAC/lightbox
The repository of lightbox
.UE
